Characterisation Programs
-------------------------

The aim of a characterisation exercise is to ensure that all measurable
parameter results can be provided to the design team who can then confirm that
the devices predicted datasheet performance can be met over the specified 
voltage and temperature range when process tolerances have been taken into
account.

In order to mimic the process spreads that can occur during long term production
runs of a mature product, batches of silicon with differing properties are
fabricated.

Usually three lots of silicon are processed, slow-slow, typical-typical and 
fast-fast which relate to the p/n type transistor gains respectively.
The most common parameters varied are the effective channel lengths (Leff's) or
thresholds (Vt's) of the transistors.

Confirmation that the transistors have been fabricated to the desired 
requirements is provided by the measurements performed on test structures 
located at designated sites on each wafer. These also allow the wafers to
classed as pass or fail in production runs at this parametric probe stage.

Characterisation is performed on these split lots using a dedicated 
characterisation program which incorporates the required routines.

It is advisable to obtain characterisation results at a pin level
for initial characterisation. This will immediately highlight the slowest
signals within a bus in the case of ac parameters or problems with
input switching and output levels for that of dc parameters.
The level of characterisation could be controlled by surrounding the
respective code in the characterisation program by software switches, which
will then allow a high (bus) or low (signals within a bus) to be measured.

The characterisation program is not a test program, its function is to perform
measurements and return values for observation. However, expected limits of the 
measured parameters can be tested for, thus highlighting parameters of concern.
Thus in the description of the characterisation routines, the measured value
should be recorded for subsequent analysis and may also be compared against
predicted limits.

Probe Test Program
------------------
The probe program usually excludes ac parametric tests and dynamic idd tests.
These tests require a setup capable of supporting high frequency signals and
this is difficult to achieve when probe load board impedances are realised.

Final Test Program
------------------
The final test program will test all the parameters as detailed in the 
devices respective datasheet.

QA Test Program
---------------

The QA test program should contain all the tests as per the Final Test program.
It is common to use the QA test location as a means of ensuring that all the
tests have been performed correctly in the final test flow and the parameter
test limits are usually set to the actual datasheet values with reduced or even
no guardbanding applied.

Guardbanding
------------

It is common practice to apply guardbanding on both ac and dc parameters to
account for the possible tolerances and inaccuracies of the test system.
Where stated in this document, 'datasheet' values should include the guardband
offsets where deemed necessary.

Characterisation and Test Program Routines
==========================================

Active Signal Open Circuit Continuity Test.
...........................................

This test is performed to check that an open circuit does not exist in the paths
between each tester channel source and its respective die pad termination.

A common method of performing this test on CMOS devices is to check for the
existence of the protection diodes of input stages and the inherent protection
diodes of input/output combination stages.

It is advisable to check that both diodes (where applicable) to Vdd and Vss are 
present as this also ensures that the protection structures are at least 
present.

The procedure for performing active signal open circuit continuity is :

   - Program Vdd and Vss to OV.

   - Initially program all active pins to 0V.

   - Configure the programmable measuring unit to force current and measure
     voltage, with the voltage clamped to +/-1V limiting its range should an
     open circuit exist.

   - To check continuity via the diode to Vdd, force +100uA into the pad under
     test and check the diode turn on voltage to be less than an expected upper
     limit of 0.9V, for example.
     A voltage greater than this signifies an open circuit and will usually
     be realised by the returned voltage being equal to the programmed clamp
     voltage i.e 1V in this case.
 
   - To check continuity via the diode to Vss, force -100uA into the pad under
     test and check the diode turn on voltage to be less than an expected
     lower limit of -0.9V, for example.
     A voltage less than this signifies an open circuit and will usually
     be realised by the returned voltage being equal to the programmed clamp
     voltage i.e 1V in this case.

Note that 100uA is used as an example current and any suitable low current that
will not cause damage to the protection diodes can be used.

Active Signal Short Circuit Continuity Test.
............................................

This test is performed to check that a short circuit does not exist in the paths
between each tester channel source and its respective die pad termination to
any other active signals or supply lines.

A common method of performing this test on CMOS devices is to check for the
existence of the protection diodes of input stages and the inherent protection
diodes of input/output combination stages.

The procedure for performing active signal short circuit continuity is :

   - Program Vdd and Vss to OV.

   - Initially program all active pins to 0V.

   - Configure the programmable measuring unit to force current and measure
     voltage, with the voltage clamped to +/-1V limiting its range should an
     open circuit exist. The current is obviously limited to 100uA by its
     programmed constant current force value.

   - To check continuity via the diode to Vdd, force +100uA into the pad under
     test and check the diode turn on voltage to be greater than an expected
     lower limit of 0.2V, for example.
     A voltage less than this signifies that a short circuit exists.

   - It is not necessary to repeat for this test for diodes to Vss as all short
     circuits will be have been detected in this test.

Note that 100uA is used as an example current and any suitable low current that
will not cause damage to the protection diodes or shorted circuitry can be used.

Supply Continuity Tests
.......................

This is a coarse test to establish that power is being supplied to the device by
monitoring the Idd supply current which must lie within an expected range.
Too high a current measurement signifies possible short circuits whilst too low
a current points to possible open circuits.

It is recommended that the die be tested under the following operating conditions :

   - Nominal Vdd.
   - Relaxed Clock Frequency.
   - Relaxed timings.
   - Relaxed input levels.
   - Relaxed output levels.
   - No output loading.
   - Use the specified Idd dynamic test pattern module.

Gross Functional Test
.....................

This test is performed to check that the fabricated device is logically correct.

It is recommended that the die be tested under the following operating conditions :

   - Nominal Vdd.
   - Relaxed Clock Frequency.
   - Relaxed timings.
   - Relaxed input levels.
   - Relaxed output levels.
   - No output loading.
   - Check the functionality of all test pattern modules.

The test patterns will generally be supplied as independent modules which will
improve failure analysis at this stage by highlighting common logic fail areas
of the device.

Leakage Tests
.............

These tests are performed to check the input and tri-state pad impedances.

In general, leakage paths exist between the pad and the substrate via the thin
oxide and protection diode structures. 
In a CMOS process, the P- wells are at ground whilst the N- wells are at Vdd,
hence to check for leakage through n-channel transistor oxide, it is necessary  
to force the n-channel gate to a logic '1' level, whilst the reverse is true for a p-channel transistor.

The following should be noted when performing leakage tests :

   - Erroneous values can be obtained if leakage tests are performed when
     the current being drawn through the internal Vss and Vdd lines causes a
     significant change in their potential across the respective conductor
     length.

     In the case of an input pad being forced to logic '0', if its internal 
     Vss rail potential increases to the protection diode turn-on voltage,
     then forward diode current will flow and summate to the true leakage
     current.
     The same is true for an input pad being forced to logic '1' and its
     internal
     Vdd rail decreases by a protection diode turn on voltage.
  
     This effect can sometimes exist if the device contains logic which
     requires initialisation and refreshing to maintain its internal logic
     levels.
     If the internal nodes are not initialised to good levels, then high
     transient current can flow, causing the reduction in Vdd and increase in
     Vss internal rail levels.
     It may be necessary to keep the clock running at the refresh rate.  

   - If the input stages contain high current drivers, then a potential gradient
     may exist if for example all n-channel transistors are turned on in a bus
     with insufficient Vss connections.

The procedures for measuring input leakage will differ for each device, but
in general the following strategy is recommended.

Input Pad Leakage :

   - where specified, configure the device into a known state by running the
     relevant test pattern.

   - to perform input leakage low (IIL) measurements, set the pin under test
     to Vss and all other remaining input pins, if possible, to VddMax and
     measure the current being drawn.

   - to perform input leakage high (IIH) measurements, set the pin under test
     to VddMax and all other remaining input pins, if possible,  to Vss and
     measure the current being drawn.

   - if the effects as described above are apparent, it is permissible after
     justification to adjust the forcing values such that true leakage 
     measurements are obtained i.e increase the VIL or decrease VIH.

   - if pullup or pulldown resistors are inherent in the pad, then an adjustment
     to the leakage test limits must be made, taking into account the additional
     current drawn.

Tri-State Pad Leakage :

   - where specified, configure the device into a known state by running the
     relevant test pattern. Here the pattern must either terminate or stop
     at a point where the tristate pads are known to be high impedance.

   - the same processes are then repeated as in the Input Pad Leakage test.
  
DC Threshold Tests
..................

It is recommended that the dc threshold tests be implemented in the probe
program if the signal integrity is such that the programmed levels truly exist
at the die pads. 
If this is not the case, then the test should not be included as this will
possibly result in false yield loss.

It is recommended that the die be tested under the following operating
conditions :

   - Minimum, Nominal and Maximum Vdd.
   - Relaxed Frequency.
   - Relaxed timings.
   - Datasheet input  voltage levels.
   - Datasheet output detector levels 
   - Datasheet output current loads.
   - Use all functional test pattern modules.

DC Voltage Input Threshold Characterisation
...........................................

For the device to operate correctly, it is necessary to supply input stimulus
that meet the datasheet specification.
This means that input low levels must be less than or equal to the specified 
VIL value and the input high levels must greater than or equal to the specified 
VIH value.

Provided these conditions are met and the device is logically correct, then the
device will function correctly. 
The internal switching level is designed such that it provides a noise
guardband.

It is possible to measure the value of this internal switching point by
varying the VIL (Voltage Input Low) and VIH (Voltage Input High) levels 
independently until the device fails, at which point the input structures
have failed to translate the level at the input to a correct internal level.

It is recommended that the device be characterised under the following operating
conditions :

   - Minimum, Nominal and Maximum Vdd.
   - Relaxed Frequency.
   - Relaxed timings.
   - VIH relaxed, VIL varied.
   - VIL relaxed, VIH varied.
   - Relaxed output detector levels.
   - Relaxed output current loads.
   - Use all characterisation test pattern modules.

The characterisation strategy is to measure each dc parameter in isolation,
tightening only the conditions of the dc parameter under investigation, with all
other dc parameters relaxed, thereby focusing on this parameter as the most
probable failure mechanism.

This approach allows independent modular characterisation routines to be
developed, debugged and run. It should yield a consistent set of parameter
values, as in general, each device to be characterised is exercised by exactly
the same set of stimuli.

The moving of levels may be performed in a manner which is most suited to the
target testers capabilities and should aim to reduce the time taken to achieve
a final value e.g in general a binary search routine is faster in returning a
result that a linear search routine.

It is envisaged that the inputs of the device will be partitioned into signals 
and buses, which can be programmed to independent voltage levels.

The input swings of these signals and buses will also be programmable by 
setting of the VIL level, the minimum low level that the signal will reach, and
VIH, the maximum high level that the signal will reach.

With this flexibility it is possible to maintain the same characterisation
strategy as is applied in the determination of ac timing parameters i.e 
only the signal/bus dc parameter under determination is tightened whilst all
other dc and ac signals are kept relaxed.

Maximum VIL Determination
.........................

The maximum VIL value is determined by relaxing the VIH level to Vdd for
all signals, including that currently being characterised, then increasing the
VIL level from Vss for the signal currently being characterised.

At every VIL increment, the characterisation test patterns are run and provide
a pass/fail result, the last passing value gives the maximum VIL level that can
be used for the device under test.

Minimum VIH Determination
.........................

The minimum VIH value is determined by relaxing the VIL level to Vss for
all signals, including that currently being characterised, then decreasing the
VIH level from Vdd for the signal currently being characterised.

At every VIH decrement, the characterisation test patterns are run and provide
a pass/fail result, the last passing value gives the minimum VIH level that can
be used for the device under test.

Static VOL/VOH
..............

This test is optional and can be used to provide additional information with
regards to the output drive capabilities.

If dedicated VOL and VOH patterns are available, then these simplify the process
of static VOH/VOH testing. Here the pattern has been written to set all or a
large proportion of the outputs to either highs or lows at the end of the
pattern.
 
The procedure for measuring VOL is :

   - configure the programmable measuring unit to force current and measure
     voltage.
   - set the force current to the IOL datasheet value and set the clamp to 2V.
   - run the appropriate test pattern to the stop vector.
   - connect the programmable measuring unit to each pin under test and compare
     the measured values with datasheet VOL value.
     Fail if greater than VOL value.

The procedure for measuring VOH is :

   - configure the programmable measuring unit to force current and measure
     voltage.
   - set the force current to the IOH datasheet value and set the clamp to 3V.
   - run the appropriate test pattern to the stop vector.
   - connect the programmable measuring unit to each pin under test and compare
     the measured values with datasheet VOH value.
     Fail if less than VOH value.

It is recommended that the die be tested under the following operating
conditions :

   - Minimum, Nominal and Maximum Vdd.
   - Relaxed Clock Frequency, clock static during the measurement.
   - Relaxed timings.
   - Relaxed input voltage levels.
   - Datasheet output detector levels.
   - Datasheet output current loadings.
   - Use VOL and VOH test pattern modules (if available), else a suitable
     module e.g JTAG which sets all outputs high and low.

Static IOSL/IOSH Tests
......................

These tests are very similar to the VOH and VOL tests and are not always
specified. IOSL and IOSH are the sink and source currents of an output stage
when its drivers are shorted to Vdd or Vss respectively. 

The procedure for measuring IOSL is :

   - configure the programmable measuring unit to force voltage and measure
     current.
   - set the force voltage to appropriate Vdd value and set the clamp to around
     twice the datasheet current.
   - run the appropriate test pattern to the stop vector.
   - connect the programmable measuring unit to each pin under test and compare
     the measured current values with datasheet IOSL value.
     Fail if less than the IOSL value.

The procedure for measuring IOSH is :

   - configure the programmable measuring unit to force current and measure
     voltage.
   - set the force voltage to Vss and set the clamp to around twice the
     datasheet current.
   - run the appropriate test pattern to the stop vector.
   - connect the programmable measuring unit to each pin under test and compare
     the measured values with datasheet IOSH value.
     Fail if less than the IOSH value.

It is recommended that the die be tested under the following operating
conditions :

   - Minimum, Nominal and Maximum Vdd.
   - Relaxed Clock Frequency, clock static during the measurement.
   - Relaxed timings.
   - Relaxed input voltage levels.
   - Relaxed output detector levels.
   - Datasheet output short circuit loadings to each pin in turn.
   - Use VOL and VOH test pattern modules (if available), else a suitable
     module e.g JTAG which sets all outputs high and low.

Idd quiescent
.............

It is difficult to theoretically ascertain the exact device state that produces
the maximum Idd quiescent current and hence it is recommended that some analysis
of the current drawn at specific vectors be performed to establish a set of
points at which the highest Idd quiescent currents are being drawn. 
These then form a set of measurement points for the Idd quiescent determination.

The procedure for measuring Idd quiescent is :

   - configure the programmable measuring unit to force voltage and measure
     Idd current.
   - run the pattern to each defined 'stop' point in the vectors and measure
     the quiescent Idd current each time.
   - fail if any measured value greater than the datasheet Idd quiescent value.

It is recommended that the die be tested under the following operating
conditions :

   - Maximum Vdd.
   - Relaxed Frequency, clock static during the measurement.
   - Relaxed timings.
   - Relaxed input levels, VIL = VSS, VIH = VDD.
   - Relaxed output detector levels.
   - No output loadings.
   - Use defined points within each functional pattern module.

AC Parameter Tests
..................

It may be necessary to split up the testing of the ac parameters into 
groups. This may be because of the inability to test every parameter in a 
particular timeset or for convenience due to the quantity of parameters being
tested.

It is arguable as to what input levels and output current loadings should apply
during the ac tests so the worse case conditions have been stated.
There may be a necessity to relax some of the levels and loadings due to the
tester environment as is generally the case.
 
It is recommended that the die be tested under the following operating
conditions :

   - Minimum, Nominal and Maximum Vdd.
   - Maximum Datasheet Clock Frequency.
   - Datasheet timings.
   - Datasheet input voltage levels.
   - Datasheet output detector levels.
   - Datasheet output current loadings.
   - Use all functional test pattern modules.

AC Parameter Characterisation
.............................

One of the major exercises to be performed on a device is the ac parameter
characterisation.

To ease this exercise a separate device specific document has been written
for all ARM devices that require ac parametric characterisation.

The reader should refer to the respective document which describes in detail 
the procedures for the parameters determination.

Characterisation test patterns will be supplied in pairs of independent modules
aimed at characterising sets of parameters. 

The pair of modules characterise parameters which are referenced to the falling
and rising edges of the primary clock.

The most significant feature which requires two patterns to be supplied is the
necessity to measure the an outputs propagation delay time in one pattern and
its hold time in the other.

It should be noted that timeset switching is not provided within the patterns
and it is realised that output propagation delays and hold times could be
measured within the same pattern if this facility were available.

Idd dynamic.
............

This test measures the dynamic Idd current of the device whilst it is running
a pattern which is typical of its normal mode of operation.
The chosen pattern is looped while one or more Idd measurements are recorded and
averaged.

It is recommended that the die be tested under the following operating
conditions :

   - Maximum Vdd.
   - Maximum Datasheet Clock Frequency.
   - Relaxed timings.
   - Relaxed input voltage levels.
   - Relaxed output detector levels.
   - No output current loadings.
   - Use the specified dynamic Idd test pattern module.

