Binning
-------

Each test program must be capable of supplying high level failure analysis as
per the <example> below. 

Bin       Category
---       --------
1         Pass 
2          --
3          --
4          --
5          --
6          --
7         Engineering Data
8         FAIL : Idd Static
9         FAIL : DC Threshold Test.
10        FAIL : Gross Functional
11        FAIL : AC Parametric.
12        FAIL : Idd Dynamic
13        FAIL : Leakage.
14        FAIL : Continuity - Open.
15        FAIL : Continuity - Short.

Software Bins
-------------

A lower level of <soft> binning should be adopted to provide a breakdown of the
the high level bin category e.g in the case ac parametric fails, a set of
<soft> bins will be assigned to monitor setup, hold, clockwidths, propagation
delay fails etc. 
The devices may be physically binned as a consequence of the ac fail and each
software bin is accordingly updated if it has failed.
This mechanism provides vital post 'production' yield analysis information,
thereby releasing the tester and allowing offline failure analysis to be
targeted to the failing pins/areas.

Parameter Sampling
------------------

A <coarse> monitor of the fabrication process can also be performed at the
final test stages. By selecting a subset of device parameters, ac and dc,
then for each parameter a search routine is activated and the determined value
recorded. The search routines are only activated every 1 in 'n' devices
(e.g 1 in 50), so as not to adversely effect the overall test time.
The sampled values are averaged on a continuous basis.

Test Flows
==========

Test Flow Recommendations
-------------------------

The recommended Test/Burnin/QA flow is :

   - Pre Burnin Hot Test

   - Burnin

   - Post Burnin Hot Test

   - Post Burnin Cold Test

   - QA Hot  Test

   - QA Cold Test


Justification should be provided for not performing hot and/or cold testing,
each of which can act as an efficient screen for leakage and threshold fails
respectively, besides the more dominant ac/dc failure mechanisms.


Reliability
===========

Accelerated Testing
-------------------

Accelerated testing is performed to screen out early life fails, more generally
referred to as 'infant mortality', so that a very low steady state failure rate
occurs throughout the normal life expectancy and operation of the surviving
devices.

There are five common stresses used to accelerate device failure mechanisms :
temperature, voltage, current, humidity, and temperature cycling.

Temperature Acceleration
------------------------

Many of the mechanisms that cause failure are chemical or physical processes
that can be accelerated by temperature. 
Some failure mechanisms are accelerated by increased temperature whilst others
require reduced temperatures. 
It is imperative that the device failure rate be studied at 'all' extremes of
temperature.

Voltage Acceleration
--------------------

Voltage or electric fields accelerate failure mechanisms caused by dielectric
breakdown, interface charge accumulation, charge injection and corrosion.

Current Acceleration
--------------------

Increased current levels are used primarily as a method of accelerating 
failures caused by electromigration in the metallic conductors, and this type
of acceleration is usually performed on current-controlled test structures.

Humidity-Temperature Acceleration
---------------------------------

Water vapour quickly permeates plastic packaging materials, transporting
contaminants from the its outer surface, leaching impurities from the 
the plastic package itself and finally presenting these to the chip surface.
Diffusion of this contaminated water vapour through the passivation layer
is comparatively slower, but is quickened by any defects or cracks in it and
around the exposed cutout bond pad areas.
Once the water vapour reaches the metalisation level of the chip,
electrochemical corrosion quickly takes place, resulting in a metallisation
failure.
Increasing the partial pressure of the water vapour and its ambient temperature
increases rate-limiting diffusion of the water vapour through the passivation 
layer.

Temperature Cycling
-------------------

Temperature cycling is used to accelerate mechanical failure of the chip.
Here the devices are subjected to extreme negative and positive temperatures on
a cyclic basis. 
The types of failure that can occur are be bondwire shears from the bond pad,
package cracks, lid seal fails, passivation cracks etc. The device is not usually under electrical bias in these tests.

General Exercise Strategy
-------------------------

The devices under test should be tested prior to the exercise entry and at well
defined read points e.g 0Hrs, 168Hrs, 500Hrs and 1000Hrs by removal from their
boards and subsequently tested/characterised using a standard production line
tester. 

The devices should be tested in accordance within the inhouse specified 'window'
time. This aims to reduce the effect of devices 'recovering' if parameters have
shifted as a consequence of the applied stress.

The devices should be tested using the standard production line test program,
usually the QA version, to provide a go-nogo test report and providing high level failure analysis at each readpoint. 

It is also advised that the devices be fully characterised, by use of the
characterisation program, at each read point to monitor fabrication process
parametric shifts, should they occur during the exercise. 
The characterisation program should be capable of providing a minimum set of ac
and dc parametric data.

A record of the yield, failure analysis and significant parametric shifts should
be made at each read point.

General Board Design and Output Loading Considerations
------------------------------------------------------

Signal Integrity
................ 

Care must be taken in the design of the active signal generators to ensure that
they are capable of meeting the minimum rise/fall times and attaining the
specified input switching levels at each device as defined in the respective
Data Sheet when the boards are fully loaded.
Termination resistors may be required to reduce reflections and hence improve
overall signal integrity on the distributed clock lines.

Current Limiting Resistors
..........................

Current limiting resistors should always be used where signals are connected to
a fixed logic level i.e between the pin and Vdd/Vss supply line.
In the event of input circuitry breaking down in some manner and causing a short
internal to the device, providing a path to one of the supply rails, the current
drawn is now limited by the inclusion of these resistors and hence the
exercising will continue i.e the power supply will not current limit.

Outputs Unloaded
................

If the device outputs are unloaded, the peripheral output drivers will only be
stressed by very short transition currents that occur as the 'p' and 'n'
transistors switch state.
In the steady state between transitions, there will be only leakage current
flowing through the drain-source regions of both the 'p' and 'n' transistors.

Its advantage is that there is less space taken up by passive components on the 
board and less current drawn from the power supply.

Resistive Loading
.................

If the device outputs are resistively loaded, the resistor should be connected to a midrail voltage and its value chosen such the rated IOL/IOH flows.

This is usually not realistic as it requires another supply to be provided
capable of sinking/sourcing the currents and piping this around the board or
doubling the amount of resistors by providing a potential divider for each
output.
 
A compromise is to split the output pin resistor connections between the Vdd and
Vss lines. This results in those pins connected to ground via the resistors
having their 'p' channels stressed more than their corresponding 'n' channels
and vice versa.

Resistive loads result in transition currents in both 'p' and 'n' transistors
and steady state currents to flow in either or both of the 'p' and 'n'
transistors dependent on their configuration.

The disadvantage is that the passive components require board space and must be
capable of withstanding the high temperatures for very long durations. There is also the additional power requirements to consider. 

Capacitive Loading
..................

If the device outputs are capacitively loaded, then both the 'p' and 'n'
transistors in the peripheral output drivers are exercised by internal and
external transition currents in a more even manner.

The initial capacitor current will be limited only by the on resistance of the
respective source/sink transistor and hence would be higher than that
experienced in a resistive setup. The current will however decay as the
capacitor charges/discharges.

The disadvantages are the cost of capacitors that can withstand the high
temperatures and the board space used.

                     <Advanced RISC Machines>
